1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:05,760
Hey everyone.  We've seen how incentive misalignment causes issues between divisions and the firm.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:11,360
And this arose due to the profit and cost center misalignment coordination costs and attention misallocation.

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:16,800
So now that we understand the problem, how do we fix it?

4
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,680
How do we stop these smart, capable teams from making decisions that good for them?

5
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:26,600
Because they become profit centers and it looks good on their bottom line, they're bad for the business.

6
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,440
All right, so this isn't about policies or pricing then.

7
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,200
It's about our organizational design.

8
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:35,880
And we have three strategic levers that we can pull.

9
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:39,000
We have pricing incentives and decision rights.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,840
Let's look at each one of these.

11
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:45,000
First up here, centralized transfer pricing.

12
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:54,480
Instead of letting divisions negotiate prices with each other, that can often lead to turf wars and inefficiencies, let's have corporate headquarters step in.

13
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:00,240
They can set a standard transfer price based on the opportunity cost of the asset.

14
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:14,000
In nextec's case, that means pricing the battery based on what the division could earn from external buyers, but on the value it creates internally for the device division.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,600
This ensures that decisions are made based on what's best for the firm, not individual units.

16
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,240
Here we can look at opportunity cost based pricing.

17
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:27,800
This can be aligning with our economic theory.

18
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:35,560
The optimal price reflects the next best alternative, use of the resource, not arbitrary markups.

19
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:46,760
This keeps assets flowing to their highest value use and avoids the value destruction that happens when internal prices are distorted.

20
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,320
Next we look at compensation.

21
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,560
Right now, each division is paid based on its own profits.

22
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:55,240
That sounds kind of fair, right?

23
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:59,880
But in reality, what happens is it fuels competition between teammates.

24
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:09,480
What if instead, both the battery and devices division earn bonuses only if Next Tech's overall profit increase.

25
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:15,960
Suddenly the battery team cares if its pricing hurts product sales because it hits their bonus too.

26
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,960
This is a shift from local optimization to global alignment.

27
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:32,920
By tying performance metrics to firmwide outcomes, stuff like total profit, stock price or key performance indicators, we encourage cross division cooperation.

28
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:35,720
It's Basically Incentive Theory 101.

29
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,800
People do what they're paid to do.

30
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,920
You change the rewards and behavior will follow.

31
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,640
But, and again, this is important.

32
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:47,240
This approach can also dilute accountability.

33
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:55,520
If everyone is measured on a big shared number, some may coast while others carry the load.

34
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,960
That's known as a free free rider problem.

35
00:02:58,120 --> 00:02:59,240
And it's a big risk.

36
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:01,800
So execution here matters.

37
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:07,000
We need performance metrics that balance individual impact with corporate success.

38
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:14,080
Third, and maybe the most radical fix here is reassign our decision rights.

39
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:20,080
What if the device's division was allowed to source batteries externally?

40
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,440
If the internal price wasn't competitive.

41
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,040
Now the battery division must price fairly or risk losing the deal.

42
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:34,160
So it's kind of like injecting a little external market discipline into internal decisions.

43
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:37,080
This approach doesn't require top down micromanagement.

44
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:44,080
It uses incentives and choice to push internal players towards value, maximizing behavior.

45
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:46,040
So this is some cosian logic.

46
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:49,320
Here we're going to look at a solution that involves cosian logic.

47
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:58,000
Whenever transactions cost inside the firm are higher than market alternatives, markets outperform hierarchies.

48
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:08,640
Given divisions the right to buy or sell externally when it makes sense disciplines inefficient pricing without corporate intervention.

49
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:10,480
All right, but beware here.

50
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,920
Too much autonomy can create fragmentation.

51
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:22,080
You risk losing scale advantages or create brand inconsistencies if every division operates like its own mini company.

52
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:30,240
So again, the solution only works when you've established clear boundaries, controls and strategic alignment.

53
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:33,520
Truth is here, there really is no silver bullet.

54
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:41,880
Each of these solutions has cost, has benefits, has trade offs, and the right choice depends on the company's size, structure, culture, etc.

55
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:45,320
So what are we looking at here?  Central pricing.

56
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,120
It's great for coordination, but can be rigid.

57
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,120
Shared incentives.

58
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:54,360
It fosters unity, but may demotivate top performers.

59
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:57,960
And decision rights empowers teams.

60
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:01,240
But it's a risky strategic drift.

61
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:07,880
So the key here is to view organizational design not as a static structure, but as a dynamic system.

62
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:16,200
Here, this is one where pricing and incentives and authority evolve together to support the firm's strategic goals.

63
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:19,160
Let's choose the right fix here.

64
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:22,280
All right, so we've already looked at three promising solutions here.

65
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:28,680
Centralized transfer pricing time pay to company wide performance and reassigning those decision rights to internal buyers.

66
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:31,960
In reality though, there's no one size fits all.

67
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:36,840
Now for our fast scaling tech firm, what we have to do is look at potential solutions.

68
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,240
But those solutions may not work with a giant conglomerate.

69
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:47,560
And so we have to look at this from a firm level and then take a look at the trade offs that are involved.

70
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:51,160
Now let's look at transfer centralized transfer pricing first.

71
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,480
We know that it offers clarity and consistency.

72
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,040
No more battles over who's charging what.

73
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:00,640
We're just standardized in internal price based off of opportunity costs.

74
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:03,680
Now this aligns decisions with what's best for the whole company.

75
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,880
It reduces conflict and lobbying between divisions.

76
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:10,240
And it ensures product flows at their highest value use.

77
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,120
But here's a downside.

78
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:18,920
Central pricing often depends on limited, biased or delayed information.

79
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:26,840
Headquarters might not fully understand any fast changing local conditions, especially in global or fast moving markets.

80
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:36,120
So this is a classic trade off between centralized authority and local knowledge as framed by Friedrich Hayek and later organizational theorists.

81
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:40,080
The more removed HQ is from transaction.

82
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:43,720
And so this is a classic trade off between centralized authority and local knowledge.

83
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:51,280
All right, so what we see here, the more removed HQ is from the transaction, the harder it becomes to set efficient prices.

84
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:55,760
Especially when internal players manipulate data to win favorable terms.

85
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:04,000
So if your corporate center isn't equipped with real time trustworthy data, you might be centralizing the illusion of control, but no actual strategy.

86
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:06,320
Let's look at our second approach.

87
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:08,720
Tying bonuses to company wide results.

88
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:11,920
This is going to send a powerful message we can win together.

89
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:17,880
Pros here is that it encourages operational encourages cooperation across divisions.

90
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,920
It reduces turf wars and destructive internal competition.

91
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:24,760
And it aligns division behavior with shareholder value.

92
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,160
But we have to watch out for this thing called free riding.

93
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:39,680
If one division performs exceptionally well, while others under deliver, top performers may feel their hard work is diluted and unrewarded.

94
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,720
This is known as the multi agent problem.

95
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:52,880
In economics, when multiple agents are rewarded based on a shared outcome, individual effort becomes harder to monitor and less directly tied to the reward.

96
00:07:53,360 --> 00:08:02,960
Over time, high performance may disengage or high performers may disengage unless the system includes differentiated performance signals.

97
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:16,200
So here, to succeed with shared incentives, you need a culture of mutual accountability, peer review and sometimes team based metrics that combine division results with cross functional goals.

98
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:24,160
Our last and third fix tier is giving internal buyers the right to choose external vendors.

99
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:26,680
Here this can be game changing for performance.

100
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:31,720
It injects market discipline into internal processes so we can see the pros and cons.

101
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:39,520
Keeps internal pricing honest, reduces our HQ intervention and encourages divisions to earn each other's business.

102
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,360
But this isn't risk free.

103
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:44,680
It allows internal buyers to get to the market.

104
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:50,960
And that can lead to fragmentation where brand consistency and data integration or innovation pipelines suffer.

105
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:54,640
So this returns to Coase's theory of the firm.

106
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:06,160
Firms exist because they can coordinate activities more efficiently than markets, but only when the transaction costs are lower inside than outside.

107
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:13,200
When you push too much decision making to the market, you may lose economies of scale, shared learning or brand coherence.

108
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:33,800
So what we look at with NexTech, if device division switches to an external battery supplier with slightly lower prices but inconsistent quality or product reliability, what we could see is that the device may begin to decline, its quality may begin to decline and that could damage the company's reputation.

109
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:43,000
So this solution really works best when products are modular and supply markets are mature and internal divisions are held to competitive standards.

110
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:47,720
All Right.  So let's make a right choice and look at a strategic fit here.

111
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:51,640
So ultimately the best solution depends on what our strategic priorities are.

112
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:57,080
If we're optimizing for efficiency and alignment, then centralized transfer pricing may work.

113
00:09:57,240 --> 00:10:03,240
If our top priority is collaboration and culture, shared incentives might be the best option.

114
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:09,960
If we operate in fast moving markets, flexible decision rights might unlock the speed and innovation that we need.

115
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:14,200
This reflects what organizational economists call the contingency approach.

116
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:20,120
There is no universally best structure, only best fits the right architecture.

117
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:27,200
Depends on your business model, market conditions, technological complexity and cultural norms.

118
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:35,680
So don't just ask which fix is best, instead ask which trade offs can we live with?

119
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:39,360
And ask where's the real friction in our system?

120
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:41,920
And above all, design with intent.

121
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:46,480
Because the structure you choose will shape the behavior that you get.

